1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,444
Maman, Why Is It Night?

2
00:00:03,444 --> 00:00:29,840
Before dawn on March 8 the Israeli Air Force bombed Iran’s oil facilities for the first time in the war. Four people were killed. Black smoke from the storage tanks spread over the capital. The sun came up and it did not get light. There were fears of fuel shortages, of poisoned water and soil, of damage to people’s lungs. The war’s targets were moving from military sites to the country’s livelihood.

3
00:00:30,440 --> 00:01:00,964
The sun came up. It did not get light. Maryam, a nurse, came off the night shift and stood at the ward window. The sky over Tehran was the color of ink. The emergency room had been full since four. Not people hurt by shrapnel. People coughing. Children with asthma, old people with weak lungs. By midnight half the oxygen tanks were empty. Today she learned breath. The sound a person makes when they cannot breathe.

4
00:01:01,564 --> 00:01:29,856
In the hospital dorm, seven-year-old Nariman had her chin on the windowsill. “Maman, it’s morning. Why is it night?” “Because oil is burning. A very big oil tank. It’ll take a few days.” “So it’s night for a few days?” Nariman drew a sun with her finger on the fogged glass. One circle, eight lines. A bomb hurts only the people it hits. Smoke chooses everyone who breathes.

5
00:01:30,456 --> 00:01:34,196
Epic Fury continues in Episode 5.
